---
// Type specimen playground: real post content rendered in candidate body
// faces, switchable live. Unpublished (/lab/ = noindex, out of sitemap).
// Candidates load from Google Fonts here for comparison only; the chosen
// face gets self-hosted via fontsource like the current ones.
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Type Specimen" description="Body face candidates on real content" noindex>
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=PT+Serif:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=Source+Serif+4:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=Literata:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=Spectral:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=Lora:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=Inter:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&display=swap"
  />

  <div class="controls" data-controls>
    <div class="row">
      <span class="row-label">Face</span>
      <button data-font="'PT Serif', serif">PT Serif (old)</button>
      <button data-font="'Source Serif 4', serif">Source Serif 4</button>
      <button data-font="'Literata', serif">Literata</button>
      <button data-font="'Newsreader Variable', serif" class="active">Newsreader (chosen)</button>
      <button data-font="'Spectral', serif">Spectral</button>
      <button data-font="'Lora', serif">Lora</button>
      <button data-font="'Inter', sans-serif">Inter</button>
    </div>
    <div class="row">
      <span class="row-label">Size</span>
      <button data-size="17">17px</button>
      <button data-size="18">18px</button>
      <button data-size="19">19px</button>
      <button data-size="20" class="active">20px (chosen)</button>
      <button data-size="21">21px (old)</button>
      <span class="hint">press F to flip Newsreader / Spectral</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="specimen" data-specimen>
    <header>
      <h1>Snapshot Fees: Pricing Each Batch Auction's Liquidity</h1>
      <p class="date">July 18, 2026</p>
    </header>
    <article>
      <p>
        Batch auctions collect orders for a short window, seal it, and clear
        every crossing trade at one price. The sealing produces something
        continuous markets never have: a complete snapshot of the market's
        standing liquidity, every order in it committed and fillable at that
        instant. This piece proposes Snapshot Fees, a fee mechanism that prices
        every fill from that snapshot, recomputed each auction, by how
        directional the filled liquidity actually was. While the
        <em>Snapshot Fees</em> mechanism could work for any batch auction
        implementation, it is likely a more natural fit for a dual-flow batch
        auction.
      </p>
      <h2>Understanding Batch Auctions</h2>
      <p>
        Most electronic markets, crypto exchanges included, run on a continuous
        limit order book (CLOB). Orders rest in a book sorted by price, and when
        a new order crosses the spread it trades immediately against the best
        resting quote. Ties at the same price go to whoever arrived first. In a
        continuous limit order book, every fill, quote, and cancel is processed
        one at a time, in arrival order.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="#">Aquilina, Budish, and O'Neill</a> measured the race
        directly, using exchange message data that records the losers as well as
        the winners. In FTSE 100 stocks they found races running about once per
        minute per symbol, typically decided in five to ten microseconds,
        accounting for roughly 20% of trading volume, with the top six firms on
        one side or the other of more than 80% of them.
      </p>
      <h3>The Base Fee</h3>
      <p>
        The band <span class="chip"><i>B</i></span> is centered on the mark
        <span class="chip"><i>M</i></span>, so liquidity resting inside
        <span class="chip"><i>M</i> &pm; <i>B</i>/2</span> pays nothing. Beyond
        the band, the fee climbs at slope
        <span class="chip"><i>k</i><sub>1</sub></span> through the zone, then at
        <span class="chip"><i>k</i><sub>2</sub></span> until it reaches the cap.
        Three properties fall out of this shape:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Tight, two-sided quoting rides free inside the band.</li>
        <li>Distance from the mark is priced, not punished, until the cap.</li>
        <li>The cap bounds what any single fill can ever be charged.</li>
      </ul>
      <blockquote>
        Eliminating latency arbitrage, they estimate, would cut the market's
        cost of liquidity by 17%, about $5 billion a year in global equities.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The engine walks the eligible book at size <span class="chip"><i>D</i></span>
        per side and averages the two stopping prices. In code the walk is a
        single pass, so <code>computeMark(book, D)</code> stays
        <code>O(n)</code> in the number of price levels.
      </p>
      <pre><code>const step = clamp((reading / dOpen) ** alpha, 1 / L, L);
const dClose = Math.max(floor, dOpen * step);</code></pre>
      <p>
        Whether a venue adopts this depends on three audiences seeing their own
        stake in it: the venue that must run the machinery, the takers who
        mostly never notice it, and the makers whose economics it rewrites.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .controls {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 10;
    background: var(--bg);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
  }
  .row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0.15rem 0;
  }
  .row-label {
    width: 3rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--date-color);
  }
  .hint {
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--date-color);
  }
  .controls button {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.55rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--text-color);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .controls button.active {
    border-color: var(--article-link-hover-border);
    color: var(--heading-color);
  }

  .specimen {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 2.5rem auto 5rem;
    padding: 0 1rem;
    font-size: 19px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .specimen h1 {
    font-size: 1.7em;
    line-height: 1.15;
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  }
  .specimen .date {
    font-size: 0.72em;
    color: var(--date-color);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }
  .specimen h2 {
    font-size: 1.35em;
    margin-top: 2.2rem;
  }
  .specimen h3 {
    font-size: 1.15em;
    margin-top: 1.7rem;
  }
  .specimen .chip {
    background: var(--code-background);
    color: var(--code-color);
    padding: 0 0.25rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .specimen code {
    font-size: 0.75em;
  }
  .specimen pre {
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
</style>

<script>
  const specimen = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-specimen]");
  const controls = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-controls]");
  if (specimen && controls) {
    controls.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
      const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest("button");
      if (!btn) return;
      const row = btn.parentElement;
      if (!row) return;
      for (const b of row.querySelectorAll("button")) {
        b.classList.toggle("active", b === btn);
      }
      if (btn.dataset.font) specimen.style.fontFamily = btn.dataset.font;
      if (btn.dataset.size) {
        const px = Number(btn.dataset.size);
        specimen.style.fontSize = `${px}px`;
        specimen.style.lineHeight = px >= 21 ? "1.5" : "1.6";
      }
    });
    // flicker test between the two finalists
    document.addEventListener("keydown", (e) => {
      if (e.key !== "f" && e.key !== "F") return;
      const current = specimen.style.fontFamily;
      const next = current.includes("Spectral")
        ? "'Newsreader Variable', serif"
        : "'Spectral', serif";
      specimen.style.fontFamily = next;
      for (const b of controls.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-font]")) {
        b.classList.toggle("active", b.dataset.font === next);
      }
    });
  }
</script>
